You can now use[tasks](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-server#tasks-experimental) with Mastra` MCPServer` and` MCPClient` to run long-running tool calls in the background. The client can poll the server until the task is` completed` , then hand the result back to the agent.


While tool calls are in progress, agents are blocked and can't respond to user requests. Moving tool calls to tasks lets the agent keep responding while slow or expensive computations run in the background.


Your browser does not support the video tag.An agent keeps answering questions while a 30-day repo report runs as an MCP task, then shows the finished report as a dashboard.


Before tasks, MCP tool calls were synchronous. Each call was handled by a single HTTP request that remained open until complete, and blocked the agent loop. Mastra's existing[background tasks](https://mastra.ai/docs/harness/background-tasks) primitive could be used to free up the agent loop, but if a tool call outlasted a proxy timeout or a load balancer's idle limit, it would eventually fail.


With tasks, MCP servers can quickly respond with a task ID (which completes the HTTP request and unblocks the agent). MCP clients then use the ID to poll the server, receiving a response that includes a task status of` working` ,` input_required` ,` completed` ,` failed` , or` cancelled` . When the task completes, Mastra sends the agent a[notification signal](https://mastra.ai/docs/signals/notifications) , which reaches an active run on its next model request, instead of waiting for the agent loop to finish.


Building on our[MCP 2.0](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-mcp-2) support, tasks join[MCP events](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-mcp-events) as the latest addition to Mastra's MCP server and client primitives.


## Get started


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/mcp@latest   @mastra/core@latest
```


note


Requires` @mastra/mcp@2.7.0` and` @mastra/core@1.81.0` or later, added in[PR #28420](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/28420) .


Enable` backgroundTasks` on your Mastra instance:


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// ...
storage:   new   LibSQLStore  ({ id:   "  mastra-storage  "  , url:   "  file:./mastra.db  "   }),
backgroundTasks: { enabled:   true   }
});
```


### MCP server


Tools can be configured to run as tasks using one of the following config options:


- ` "always"` : The tool call always runs as a task.
- ` "auto"` : The tool call runs as a task if it takes longer than` syncWindowMs` (2 seconds by default).
- ` "off"` : The tool call never runs as a task. This is the default.


src/mastra/mcp/mcp-server.ts


```text
import   {   MCPServer   }   from   "  @mastra/mcp  "  ;
import   {   reportTool   }   from   "  ../tools/report-tool  "  ;


export   const   mcpServer   =   new   MCPServer  ({
id:   "  mcp-server  "  ,
// ...
tools: { reportTool },
tasks: {
tools: { reportTool:   "  always  "   }
}
});
```


Register the server on your Mastra instance:


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// ...
mcpServers: { mcpServer }
});
```


Tools that run as tasks can still use` suspend()` to ask for human input. The task pauses in an` input_required` state until the client responds:


src/mastra/tools/report-tool.ts


```text
export   const   reportTool   =   createTool  ({
// ...
resumeSchema: z.  object  ({
approved: z.  boolean  ()
}),
execute  :   async   (inputData, context)   =>   {
if   (  !  context.resumeData) {
await   context.  suspend  ?.({ message:   "  Generate a report?  "   });
return  ;
}
// ...
}
});
```


### MCP client


Set` tasks` on the` MCPClient` so it can poll tasks from the MCP server for their results, and use` inputRequests` to respond when a tool calls` suspend()` for human input:


src/mastra/mcp/mcp-client.ts


```text
export   const   mcpClient   =   new   MCPClient  ({
servers: {
mcpServer: {
url:   new   URL  (  "  http://localhost:4111/api/mcp/mcp-server/mcp  "  ),
tasks:   true  ,
inputRequests  :   async   ()   =>   {
// content.approved matches the tool's resumeSchema
return   { action:   "  accept  "  , content: { approved:   true   } };
}
}
}
});
```


Add MCP server tools to an agent using the` tools` option:


src/mastra/agents/agent.ts


```text
export   const   agent   =   new   Agent  ({
// ...
tools:   await   mcpClient.  listTools  ()
});
```


For more information and full configuration options, see:


- [MCPServer tasks](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-server#tasks-experimental)
- [MCPClient tasks](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-client#tasks-property-experimental)
- [MCPClient: Answering input requests](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-client#answering-input-requests)
- [Tool approval using suspend()](https://mastra.ai/docs/agents/human-in-the-loop#approval-using-suspend)
- [Background tasks](https://mastra.ai/docs/harness/background-tasks)
- [MCP Tasks extension (SEP-2663)](https://modelcontextprotocol.github.io/ext-tasks/specification/2026-07-28/tasks.html)


MCP tasks are still experimental — the API may change between releases.
